<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  {{> head }}
</head>
<body>
  <div class="page">
    {{> header }}
    <main class="main-content prose">
      <h1>About</h1>
      <img class="portrait" src="/img/me.jpeg" alt="Portrait of {{name}}" width="220">
      <p>Hi, I'm {{name}}. I built this app to practise building a full-stack Node.js project end to end, from server-side rendering to talking to third-party APIs.</p>
      <h2>How it works</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Server:</strong> Node.js + Express, with Handlebars templates rendered on the server.</li>
        <li><strong>API:</strong> <code>GET /api/weather?address=Taipei</code> geocodes the place, fetches the forecast and returns clean JSON.</li>
        <li><strong>Data:</strong> <a href="https://open-meteo.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open-Meteo</a> for geocoding and forecasts. No API key, so there are no secrets in the repo.</li>
        <li><strong>Performance:</strong> responses are cached in memory for 10 minutes, and upstream calls time out after 8 seconds.</li>
        <li><strong>Map (Web GIS):</strong> <a href="https://leafletjs.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Leaflet</a>, with OpenStreetMap, CARTO and Esri base layers, a GeoJSON layer of Taiwanese city temperatures (<code>/api/cities</code>) and a RainViewer rain-radar overlay. Click the map to reverse-geocode that point with OpenStreetMap Nominatim.</li>
        <li><strong>Front end:</strong> plain JavaScript, with no framework or build step. Responsive layout, and the 24-hour chart is hand-drawn SVG.</li>
      </ul>
    </main>
    {{> footer }}
  </div>
</body>
</html>
